\section{Place Contributions, Then Describe Mappings}

Levine separates the calculation of offsets within each storage class from the placement of those classes in the program address space. His text, data, and BSS example shows why the linker cannot just treat an object as one contiguous contribution and append the next one. The BSS still needs addresses even though its initial contents are not stored as bytes. His GNU linker script example describes a historical System V COFF target; the corresponding ELF distinction today is between input sections, output sections, and loadable segments \cite[chapter~4]{levine2000linkers}. Link editing assigns sections; an ELF loader ordinarily uses the program headers, not the section headers, to establish mappings.

For an output section that simply concatenates input contributions, keep a cursor in virtual addresses. If the cursor is $c$ and the next input alignment is $a>0$, start that contribution at $b=a\lceil c/a\rceil$. Its half-open interval is $[b,b+n)$ for size $n$, and the next cursor is $b+n$. Align the \emph{start of the next input}, not the length of the preceding input as though all inputs shared the same required alignment. For ELF, zero and one in \verb|sh_addralign| impose no constraint. Check addition and round-up for overflow before forming an address. The interval view also avoids the off-by-one ambiguity of saying which byte is ``the end.''

A linker script chooses input patterns, ordering, output addresses, and sometimes the program headers. Matching a section name does not change its contents or lower its input alignment; imposing a smaller alignment explicitly is an ABI decision, not a size optimization to apply blindly. GNU \texttt{ld} may place an unmatched input as an orphan, so inspect the map for unexpected contributions instead of assuming a replacement script discarded everything it did not mention. In a controlled build where every relevant section has an owner in the script, GNU \texttt{ld}'s \verb|--orphan-handling=error| can help detect a newly emitted input section. Before enabling it, account for conventional empty sections and metadata that the toolchain supplies as well as the sections carrying application data.

