\section{Lay Out a Read-Only Table and a Zero-Filled Buffer}

Assemble the first block as \texttt{layout.s} on an x86-64 ELF target. The fixtures are storage only; inspect the linked image rather than running it.

\begin{verbatim}
# Two read-only inputs with different alignment requirements.
.section .rodata.a,"a",@progbits
.p2align 2
table_a: .space 0x13, 0xa1
.section .rodata.b,"a",@progbits
.p2align 5
table_b: .space 0x0b, 0xb2
# Eight file bytes, then a buffer with no input file bytes.
.section .data.seed,"aw",@progbits
seed: .quad 17
.section .bss.scratch,"aw",@nobits
.p2align 4
scratch: .space 0x28
\end{verbatim}

Use GNU \verb|ld.bfd -T layout.ld -Map=layout.map -o layout layout.o| with this script. The \texttt{FLAGS} values designate read-only and read/write load segments, respectively.

\begin{verbatim}
PHDRS { ro PT_LOAD FLAGS(4); rw PT_LOAD FLAGS(6); }
SECTIONS {
  /* Explicit order makes the alignment gap reproducible. */
  .rodata 0x402000 : { *(.rodata.a) *(.rodata.b) } :ro
  .data   0x403000 : { *(.data.seed) } :rw
  /* Still part of the memory image, not initialized data. */
  .bss (NOLOAD) : { *(.bss.scratch) } :rw
  ASSERT(SIZEOF(.rodata) <= 0x40, "rodata budget exceeded")
}
\end{verbatim}

The first table ends at the exclusive address \verb|0x402013|. Aligning to \verb|0x20| puts the second table at \verb|0x402020|, with \verb|0x0d| padding bytes between the inputs; its end is \verb|0x40202b|. The budget therefore counts \verb|0x2b| bytes, not the \verb|0x1e| bytes of table payload. In the writable segment the seed ends at \verb|0x403008|; alignment advances the buffer start to \verb|0x403010|, and the buffer ends at \verb|0x403038|. Here \verb|p_filesz=0x8| and \verb|p_memsz=0x38|. The zero-filled part starts immediately after the file-backed eight bytes and thus includes the eight-byte gap before \texttt{scratch}.

\Cref{fig:visual-287} distinguishes padding stored in the file from the zero tail supplied in memory.

