\section{Test Alignment, File Offsets, and the Budget}

Use \verb|readelf -SW -lW layout| to distinguish the BSS section's \verb|NOBITS| type from the writable segment's file and memory lengths. Compare the addresses of labels and the section map to the half-open intervals. The \href{https://gabi.xinuos.com/elf/07-pheader.html}{ELF program header rules} require a loadable segment's file offset and virtual address to be congruent modulo the page size, and modulo the segment's stated alignment when it is greater than one. They do not require those coordinates to be equal. To find the second table in the file, add \verb|0x20| to the read-only segment's file offset, because the table starts \verb|0x20| bytes from that segment's virtual base and lies within its file-backed extent. The BSS is not represented by reading bytes at its section header's printed offset.

Change the size of \texttt{table\_a} to \verb|0x20|; the gap disappears without moving the second table. Grow it to \verb|0x21| instead; the second table starts at \verb|0x402040|, so the section size is \verb|0x4b| and the assertion must fail. The invariant is that every placed input starts on its own alignment, occupies a disjoint interval in its output section, and, for this non-overlapping load layout, has the prescribed file-backed or zero-filled memory representation. Distinguish the virtual memory address (where a reference addresses the section) from a GNU script's load memory address, which can be set with \verb|AT(...)|. For example, an embedded initialized data section may run in RAM but obtain its initial bytes from a ROM image; startup then has to copy those bytes. Changing the latter address is not a general way to change the former.

For a second boundary test, reverse the two input matches in \texttt{.rodata} while keeping the original sizes. The more strictly aligned table starts at \verb|0x402000| and ends at \verb|0x40200b|; the first table then starts at \verb|0x40200c| and ends at \verb|0x40201f|. The total payload is the same, but the padding drops to one byte. This change is legal for independent inputs like these, not for a table whose iteration order is part of the program's interface.
